<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aldhelm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 639–709</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Aldhelm of Malmesbury</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aldhelm was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> scholar, poet and churchman, abbot of Malmesbury and from 705 the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sherborne">Sherborne</a>. Writing in an elaborate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> style, he produced riddles, verse and a treatise on virginity, and he urged King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geraint-of-dumnonia">Geraint of Dumnonia</a> to adopt the Roman reckoning of Easter. He is regarded as the first major Anglo-Latin writer.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aldhelm.jpg" alt="Aldhelm" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arpingstone · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aldhelm.malmesbury.arp.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
